    "body": "Nearly 80% of U.S. fleet operators plan major fleet investments over the next six to 12 months, according to Bain & Company ’s 2026 new US Truck Study.\nWhat’s Related\nThe study, based on a survey of 210 operators, found that 79% expect their fleets to grow over the next 12 months. More than one-third expect growth of 10% or more.\nBain said the jump in demand comes as autonomy takes center stage and Tesla ramps up capacity.\nFor truck makers, Bain said the takeaway is about having trucks ready to sell. “The lesson from the last upcycle is that availability wins,” the report said.\nFuel efficiency is top factor\nFuel efficiency is the top purchase factor, cited by 45% of operators. Reliability and uptime came in at 42%, followed by total cost of ownership (TCO) at 37%. All three have led purchase decisions for years, but Bain said TCO has grown in importance. Bain said TCO has “jumped to the top spot alongside fuel efficiency” over the last three years.\nRELATED: Ranking the Top 50 Trucking Companies in the U.S.\n“Reliability, total cost of ownership, fuel efficiency and sustainability have simply become more important,” one U.S. fleet operator told Bain.\nCosts are a big reason why. Seventy percent of operators named fuel prices as their top operational challenge. Nearly half said tariffs and trade policy had a moderate or significant impact on their costs over the past year.\nFleets ready to switch brands\nNo truck maker earned an “excellent” rating for fuel efficiency in the survey. More than one-third of operators said they would switch OEMs to improve their economics. Among the largest fleets, that number rose to 41%.\n“Today’s ‘gas math’ puts every OEM’s market share at risk,” Bain said.\nElectric and autonomous truck use growing\nThe study found that 49% of operators believe electric trucks now offer better TCO than diesel, as long as enablers such as charging infrastructure are in place. With those same enablers, 67% said they would be open to buying a Tesla.\nAutonomy is also on the radar. Bain found that 73% of operators are evaluating how to use autonomous trucks in their operations.",
